---
import InfoPage from "../layouts/InfoPage.astro";
import CtaBlock from "../components/CtaBlock.astro";
import site from "../data/site.ts";
import siteData from "../data/site-data.json";

const title = "Agent Skills and MCP Tools for Salesforce";
const description =
  "How Agent Skills and the sf-intelligence MCP tools split the work: a skill decides what to ask, the read-only tools answer from your org's real metadata.";
const dateModified = "2026-10-10";

const jsonld = [
  {
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    "@id": `${site.url}/agent-skills#article`,
    headline: "Agent Skills and MCP tools for Salesforce",
    description,
    dateModified,
    inLanguage: "en",
    author: { "@id": `${site.url}/#author` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${site.url}/#org` },
    mainEntityOfPage: { "@id": `${site.url}/agent-skills#webpage` },
  },
];
---

<InfoPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/agent-skills"
  heading="Agent Skills and MCP tools"
  lede="Two layers of the same workflow. A skill tells the assistant what to do for a kind of task. The MCP tools find out what is true about your org."
  crumbs={[{ name: "Agent Skills", path: "/agent-skills" }]}
  meta="For developers"
  jsonld={jsonld}
  dateModified={dateModified}
>
  <div class="answer-first narrow">
    <span class="label">Short answer</span>
    <p>An Agent Skill is a short instruction file the assistant loads when a task matches it: which questions to ask, in what order, and how to present the result. It can’t see your org. sf-intelligence is the read-only MCP layer underneath: its tools answer each question from your org’s metadata and label the evidence. Use a skill for the routine, the tools for the facts.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="compare">
    <h2 id="compare">Side by side</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Topic</span></th><th scope="col">Agent Skills</th><th scope="col">sf-intelligence MCP tools</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">What it is</th><td>Instructions the model reads</td><td>Tools with typed inputs and outputs</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Job</th><td>Interpret the request, plan, phrase the answer</td><td>Answer one concrete question with evidence</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where it runs</th><td>In the assistant’s context</td><td>A local process reading a local snapshot</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Portable</th><td>Across orgs and assistants</td><td>Bound to one org’s snapshot</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Can it check a fact about the org?</th><td>No</td><td>Yes, and it says how sure it is</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Side effects</th><td>None</td><td>None. Read-only; live mode is opt-in</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="together">
    <h2 id="together">How they work together</h2>
    <p>Ask “is this org ready for an AI agent?” and a skill can break it into concrete questions: which fields look sensitive, what existing agents can reach, which permission sets are too broad, how much debt there is. It sends each to the right read-only tool and summarizes the results. The skill supplies the plan; the tools supply the evidence. The <a href="/use-cases/agent-readiness-audit">agent-readiness audit</a> follows this shape.</p>
    <ul class="ticks">
      <li><strong>Use a skill</strong> to encode a repeatable routine or house style: “run my pre-deploy checklist”, “give a new hire the tour”.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the tools</strong> when you need a fact: what depends on a field, who can see a record, what breaks if you delete something.</li>
      <li><strong>Use both</strong> for real work. A skill with no grounded tools will describe an org confidently and wrongly.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="ships">
    <h2 id="ships">What ships today</h2>
    <p>The npm package is the MCP server. For Claude Code, the repository also includes {siteData.skillCount} skills and {siteData.commandCount} slash commands that sit on top of the tools: routines such as admin playbooks, sharing troubleshooting and documentation, each written to keep the tools’ confidence labels and coverage notes in the answer. Read them in the <a href={`${site.github}/tree/main/.claude/skills`} rel="noopener">repository</a>.</p>
    <p>sf-intelligence doesn’t publish a separate skills package or install skills from other ecosystems.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="write">
    <h2 id="write">Writing your own skill</h2>
    <div class="prose"><ol>
      <li><strong>Describe the trigger</strong>: the kind of Salesforce question or routine it handles, so the model knows when to load it.</li>
      <li><strong>Map intent to tools</strong> and call them in a sensible order. Point the assistant at <a href="/tools#list_analyses"><code>sfi.list_analyses</code></a> and <a href="/tools#run_analysis"><code>sfi.run_analysis</code></a> for anything outside the default set.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the evidence</strong>: pass through the declared / parsed / heuristic labels and any “not checked” notes instead of flattening a hedged answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Summarize</strong> in your team’s words and format.</li>
    </ol></div>
    <p class="meta-line">The full tool surface: <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a> · <a href="/tools">Tools reference</a> · <a href="/mcp">MCP server</a></p>
  </section>

  <CtaBlock />
</InfoPage>
